// Prices (milestone 2, step 4). A settlement's price for a good, in food (person-years of
// grain), is the good's base value (goods.json) times (wanted / in store) ^ elasticity, kept
// between minFactor and maxFactor times the base value. Prices are worked out when needed,
// never stored. What a settlement wants:
//   food: enough for its store-fed people until the next harvest, plus foodReserveYears;
//   everyday and luxury goods: a year of needs (needs.json) plus reserveYears (recipes.json);
//   materials: what its craftsmen would use to make the goods it wants.
// gatherTimeValue: the food a farmer gives up by spending their whole year's gathering share
// (perPerson in resources.json) on a material; they gather it only if it is worth more.
{
  "elasticity": 1.0,
  "minFactor": 0.2,
  "maxFactor": 5.0,
  "foodReserveYears": 0.5,
  "gatherTimeValue": 0.005,

  // A settlement also wants this many times its recent sales (goods.sold: each January halved,
  // then the year's sales added; about two years of sales) of each good other than food, so a source
  // of obsidian or salt keeps enough in store to go on supplying its trade partners. Trade
  // sends at most sendShare (0.15) of a stock each season, about 0.6 of it a year, so a seller
  // can sustain yearly sales A only if 0.6 times salesWanted times about 2A exceeds A: above
  // about 0.85, stock grows with sales and demand, not this figure, limits trade.
  "salesWanted": 0.75,

  // People use less of an everyday or luxury good the dearer it is: the share of their need they
  // use is (base value / price) to this power, at most all of it. At 2, a good at five times its
  // value is used at a twenty-fifth of the need, so a rare good such as obsidian is not all used
  // up by the first villages it reaches and can travel on.
  "needElasticity": 2.0
}
